{"bill":{"id":"119-hjres-24","congress":119,"billType":"hjres","billNumber":"24","title":"Providing for congressional disapproval under chapter 8 of title 5, United States Code, of the rule submitted by the Department of Energy relating to \"Energy Conservation Program: Energy Conservation Standards for Walk-In Coolers and Walk-In Freezers\".","introducedDate":"2025-01-16","latestActionDate":"2025-05-09","latestActionText":"Became Public Law No: 119-7.","policyArea":"Energy","subjects":["Administrative law and regulatory procedures","Department of Energy","Energy efficiency and conservation","Food industry and services","Lighting, heating, cooling"],"sponsorBioguideId":"B000740","cosponsorBioguideIds":["T000478","R000619","C001116","P000605","P000609","L000583","J000301","H001093","C001103","M001233"],"committeeCodes":[],"committeeNames":[],"officialSummary":"This joint resolution eliminates new, more stringent energy conservation standards for certain walk-in coolers and freezers, including the new limits on maximum daily energy consumption for walk-in non-display doors. Under the joint resolution, walk-in non-display doors and walk-in refrigeration systems are no longer required to comply with the new standards. Specifically, the joint resolution nullifies the rule titled Energy Conservation Program: Energy Conservation Standards for Walk-In Coolers and Walk-In Freezers and submitted by the Department of Energy (DOE) on December 23, 2024. Under the rule, DOE adopted energy conservation standards for walk-in coolers and freezers to achieve the maximum improvement in energy efficiency that DOE determined was technologically feasible and economically justified. The rule required walk-in non-display doors to comply with those standards by December 23, 2027, and walk-in refrigeration systems to comply with them by December 31, 2028.","congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-joint-resolution/24","source":"congress","sourceUrl":"https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS/119/hjres/BILLSTATUS-119hjres24.xml","fetchedAt":"2026-07-29T01:30:17.199Z"},"classification":{"billId":"119-hjres-24","plainSummary":"This joint resolution would block a Department of Energy rule that set stricter energy efficiency standards for walk-in coolers and freezers used in commercial settings. The rule, finalized in December 2024, would have required walk-in refrigeration equipment to meet new energy consumption limits starting in 2027 and 2028. If passed, the resolution would nullify those standards and leave the existing, less stringent requirements in place.","industries":[{"industry":"retail-consumer","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"Grocery stores and restaurant chains operate walk-in coolers and freezers and would face different equipment compliance costs depending on whether the standards take effect."},{"industry":"manufacturing","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"Manufacturers of walk-in coolers, freezers, doors, and refrigeration systems would need to redesign products to meet the new standards or continue current designs if the rule is nullified."},{"industry":"hospitality","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"Hotels and large food-service operations use walk-in refrigeration and would be subject to the equipment standards."},{"industry":"agriculture","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"Food processing and cold-storage facilities use walk-in refrigeration equipment and would be affected by changes in equipment efficiency requirements."}],"method":"llm","model":"claude-sonnet-4-5","inputHash":"d1b1edf8787998a889e28dc8","classifiedAt":"2026-07-30T02:47:06.012Z"},"plain":{"whatItDoes":"This joint resolution would get rid of new, more stringent energy conservation standards for certain walk-in coolers and freezers. That includes the new limits on maximum daily energy consumption for walk-in non-display doors.","whoItTouches":"Anyone who buys food.","everydayEffect":"If it passed, the change would show up in what is on the shelf, and wha